{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310703,"nonce":"PCHLXSu39D/rcKGnt8pB+BiPFhQ=","title":"SIMD4f.round() returned different values on LLVM vs Cranelift (ties-away vs ties-even) so a function changed value on tier-up; fixed by ...","message":"**FIXED 2026-08-21.** `SIMD4f.round()` gave different answers depending on\nwhich tier ran it:\n\n```\nLLVM       pos 1 2 3 4    neg -1 -2 -3 -4     (llvm.round = ties AWAY from zero)\nCranelift  pos 0 2 2 4    neg -0 -2 -2 -4     (nearest      = ties to EVEN)\n```\n\nUnder tiered execution the *same function* changes value when it tiers up.\nNothing in nue used `SIMD4f.round()`, which is why it had never bitten.\n\n**The contract is now ties-to-even**, because that is the IEEE-754 default and\nthe only mode every target reaches in ONE instruction (`frintn` on ARM,\n`roundps` mode 0 on x86, `f32x4.nearest` on wasm). Ties-away would have to be\nemulated on Cranelift and wasm. LLVM was the outlier and its `llvm.round` was\nalso the slower lowering; it now emits `llvm.roundeven`. The C backend emitted\nC `round()` (ties-away) and now emits `nearbyint`.\n\n**THE GENERAL RULE this establishes: a SIMD op's semantics must be the\nINTERSECTION of what all four backends express directly, not what the richest\none offers.** Applied again immediately for the new `VectorConvert`: f32-\u003ei32\nis *saturating* (`llvm.fptosi.sat`), because wasm has only `trunc_sat` and\ncranelift only `fcvt_to_sint_sat`, while a bare LLVM `fptosi` is poison out of\nrange — an unsaturated convert would have been the same bug again.\n\n**Two more silent holes found next door and closed:**\n- `c_backend::vector_elem_c_type` ended `_ =\u003e \"i32\"`, so an i8x16 or i16x8\n  vector would get 4-byte lane arithmetic and corrupt silently. Every lane\n  width is now named and the fallthrough panics.\n- `mapping_snapshot.txt` was stale at HEAD — `4d364c98` added two mappings\n  without re-recording it, so `mapping_snapshot_unchanged` was FAILING. Ran\n  the Haxe suite that time but not `cargo test`. **Run both.**\n\nOracle: `compiler/tests/haxe/test_simd_round_ties.hx` (self-reports FAIL at\ncolumn 0, which is the only thing the harness scores).\n\nRelated: [[bugs_mir_local_scan_global_conclusion]],\n[[feedback_no_silent_dispatch_fallthrough]],\n[[perf_g1_gep_made_plain_haxe_loops_vectorise]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_simd_round_disagreed_across_tiers` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}